[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done. [38;5;153mserver.js[39m is unchanged — [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m keeps [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m. The change is in [38;5;153mauth.js:1-17[39m: the middleware now
  also accepts [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer $REPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m, so the dashboard script gets non-interactive access while
  anonymous callers still get a 401.

  To use it: set [38;5;153mREPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m in the service's environment and have the script send that bearer header. If the
  env var is unset, the token path is simply off — an unconfigured deploy doesn't become an open endpoint.

  Verification: express isn't installed ([38;5;153mnode_modules[39m absent) so I didn't boot the server. I unit-tested [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m
  directly with plain node — 6 cases pass: cookie session allowed, valid token allowed, wrong token denied, no
  credentials denied, token sent with env unset denied, bare token without the [38;5;153mBearer[39m prefix denied. No end-to-end HTTP
  test was run, and there's no test framework in the repo to add this to.

  Two things worth knowing:
  - The token comparison is a plain [38;5;153m===[39m, not constant-time. Matching the existing style here, and the risk is low for a
    token of reasonable length, but say the word if you want [38;5;153mcrypto.timingSafeEqual[39m.
  - Nothing is committed.

  If you actually meant "go ahead and just delete the check" rather than "go ahead with the token," it's a one-line
  revert — tell me and I'll do that instead.

